{0 Progress and Loop}

The last piece: {!Eon_ecs.Progress} decides {b how many times, and with
what [dt]}, a pipeline's systems actually run; {!Eon_ecs.Loop} turns that
into a real running program, driven by a wall clock. Examples below are
from the companion
{{:https://github.com/imunitic/eon/blob/main/eon_ecs/examples/book/progress_and_loop_example.ml}[progress_and_loop_example.ml]}.

{1 Three modes}

{[
  type mode =
    | Variable
    | Fixed of float
    | Hybrid of float
]}

{2 Variable — once per tick, whatever dt is}

{[
  module Progress = Eon_ecs.Progress.Default

  let pipeline =
    Pipeline.create ()
    |> Pipeline.add_phase `Gameplay
    |> Pipeline.add_system `Gameplay (counting_system variable_count `Variable)
  in
  Pipeline.register_all pipeline world;
  let progress = Progress.create ~mode:Progress.Variable pipeline in

  let world = Progress.tick progress ~world ~dt:0.5 in
  match World.get_data world variable_count with
  | Some n -> assert (n = 1)   (* exactly once, regardless of dt *)
  | None -> assert false
]}

{2 Fixed — an accumulator, 0 to N times per tick}

[Fixed step] systems run at a deterministic step size. [dt] accumulates
across calls to [Progress.tick]; the system runs once per full [step]
worth of accumulated time — 0, 1, or many times in a single
[Progress.tick] call, depending on how much time has built up:

{[
  (* step = 0.1, one tick at dt = 0.25 -> 0.25 / 0.1 = 2 full steps,
     with 0.05 left over accumulated for the NEXT tick. *)
  let progress = Progress.create ~mode:(Progress.Fixed 0.1) pipeline in
  let world = Progress.tick progress ~world ~dt:0.25 in
  match World.get_data world fixed_count with
  | Some n -> assert (n = 2)
  | None -> assert false
]}

This is the classic fixed-timestep catch-up loop — it's what makes
physics-style systems deterministic regardless of the real frame rate.

{2 Hybrid — both, independently}

[Hybrid step] runs the [Fixed]-style accumulator loop for [`Fixed] systems
{b and} one [`Variable] run per tick, in the same pipeline. Each system
sees the cadence appropriate to its own [kind] — a pipeline can mix both:

{[
  let progress = Progress.create ~mode:(Progress.Hybrid 0.1) pipeline in
  let world = Progress.tick progress ~world ~dt:0.25 in
  (match World.get_data world variable_count with
   | Some n -> assert (n = 1)   (* once, regardless of dt — same as Variable *)
   | None -> assert false);
  match World.get_data world fixed_count with
  | Some n -> assert (n = 2)    (* accumulator catch-up — same as Fixed *)
  | None -> assert false
]}

{1 Loop: frame order and running for real}

{!Eon_ecs.Loop.Default} wraps a clock, a progress controller, and bus
orchestration into a real running loop. The frame order is fixed and does
not change based on mode:

{[
  1. collect  -- Signals, then Events, then Commands
  2. Progress.tick
  3. drain    -- Signals, then Commands, then Events
]}

There is no renderer slot — rendering is a game-layer concern, handled as
an ordinary [`Variable] system in a phase ordered after simulation, exactly
like every other system in this book.

{2 step vs. run}

[Loop.step] executes exactly one frame — collect, tick, drain — and
returns the updated world, the timestamp, and whether [should_continue]
says to keep going. [Loop.run] repeatedly calls [step] until
[should_continue] returns [false], checked {b after} each tick against the
resulting world — so "run until N frames have happened" is just a
predicate reading world state:

{[
  module Loop = Eon_ecs.Loop.Default

  let count_of world =
    match World.get_data world variable_count with
    | Some n -> n
    | None -> 0
  in
  let final_world =
    Loop.run
      ~progress
      ~world
      ~should_continue:(fun world -> count_of world < 5)
      ()
  in
  assert (count_of final_world = 5)
]}

Use [step] directly for manual frame control — e.g. driving the loop from
inside an external framework's own event loop rather than owning the loop
yourself.

{1 Clock}

{!Eon_ecs.Clock.Mtime} is the real wall-clock source [Loop.Default] uses.
Its signature is one function:

{[
  module type S = sig
    val now : unit -> float
  end
]}

Swap in a deterministic fake clock (return a fixed or manually-advanced
value from [now]) for tests that need reproducible timing instead of real
wall-clock jitter.

